Purpose

Provides a centralized entry point for all external and internal consumers accessing application APIs. The API Gateway manages API traffic, security, and operational policies before requests reach the Experience API layer.

It acts as the front door of the application ecosystem, protecting backend services while providing consistent API management capabilities.

Responsibilities
Provide a single entry point for all API consumers
Perform authentication and authorization validation
Enforce API security policies
Apply rate limiting and throttling policies
Manage API routing and request forwarding
Perform request/response filtering and transformation (where appropriate)
Enforce API versioning and lifecycle management
Provide centralized monitoring, logging, and analytics
Protect backend services from excessive or malicious traffic
Support WAF integration and threat protection
Why do we need it?

Without an API Gateway, every Experience API would need to independently implement common concerns such as authentication, rate limiting, security controls, and traffic management.

This creates:

Duplicate security implementations
Inconsistent policies across APIs
Increased operational complexity
Difficulty managing API consumers at scale

The API Gateway creates a centralized governance and security boundary, allowing Experience APIs to focus on business-specific orchestration and user experience needs rather than infrastructure concerns.
